{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planet Earth is the jewel of the solar system—the \nshimmery blue oceans, the verdant green forests,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wispy whimsical cloud formations. Saturn \nwould be the only competitor for most gorgeous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12280,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet with that giant ring system. But you know \nwhat? If we could put the jewel of the Earth in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19640,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its own ring? There'd be no contest. Well, \nthere’s an extremely good chance that Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26160,
    "dDurationMs": 13160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once did have a ring system. And, you know, if  \nwe had a ring once maybe we can have one again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39320,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone—three quick \nannouncements before we jump in. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42680,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First: As you all know, with \ngreat power comes the need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46640,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for great storage and distribution. \nIf you want to spread that message,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51240,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sling on over to the merch store and snag a \ntee or sweatshirt. Link’s in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58480,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second: Your engagement experiment is working. \nLast episode’s like/comment push boosted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visibility big time. So here’s Phase 2: Let’s \nhit 50,000 likes in the first 24 hours and keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73200,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10,000+ engaged comments on each episode. It is \nweirdly powerful to do this. So let's keep going. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And third, this is the big news:\nWe are evolving. Starting this month"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of our releases every month is \ngoing to be an interview with a real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91320,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life scientists. Each month, I'll \nbring on someone who's doing work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95520,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that I consider amazing and important \nand I'll get them to spill the beans on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100440,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their own work and everything cool \nthat's happening in their field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104000,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These conversations will happen \non the community tab and will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107960,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be available for everyone a week after recording."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patreon supporters at any level will \nbe able to join the conversation live  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115800,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even ask questions and \neven ask their own questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you're hypernova teir and up \nyou can join the video call yourselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123000,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a side benefit, including this new \nformat will give the team time to up the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "production quality of regular episodes \nof which there are still two a month."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For our fist guest we are thrilled \nto be joined by Dr. Matthew Caplan,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138680,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computational nuclear astrophysicist \nand long time space time writer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We'll discuss, debate and argue about the \naspect of his work that is closest to my heart,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how real black holes feast on their \nsurrounds and grow to such enormous sizes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It'll be live on Patreon at the end of the \nmonth and on YouTube the following week."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160400,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the meantime we're collecting questions \nfor Dr. Caplan in the community tab. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you want to join us on the livestream \njoin us on Patreon at any level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ever since they were discovered by Galileo and \nthen seen for their true majesty by Christiaan  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176840,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Huygens, Saturn’s rings have been a symbol of \nserene celestial order. They seem so timeless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and eternal. But they are more transient than they \nlook. Saturn will lose its own ring system in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190920,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few hundred million years, and a hundred million \nyears or so ago an astronomer with a time machine,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or I guess a caveman with a telescope  would \nprobably have seen Saturn as a ringless yellow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ball. Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune all have \nvestigial rings—perhaps the remnants of greater  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208720,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems. There’s even a hypothesis that Mars \ngoes through cycles of having rings versus moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if planetary rings come and go, it stands \nto reason that Earth may have once had one.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now we have evidence that it really \ndid, at least according to a 2024 study  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by scientists from Monash University, in my \nown home town actually, Melbourne, Australia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233440,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we get to that evidence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s learn a bit about how planetary \nrings can form and how they can go away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start by imagining a hypothetical planet \nthat already has a ring system. Those rings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are just collections of particles; rocks, ice, \nor dust with circular orbits in a flat plane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252840,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a couple of ways this \nring could be lost. One is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particles fall onto the planet due to \nvarious factors, including gravitational,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260920,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic and even atmospheric interactions. \nThat’s actually inevitable, but can take  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a really long time. A quicker way to \nremove a ring is to turn it into moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even though the particles of a ring system \nparticles are small, they attract each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitationally and can slowly clump together into \nmassive bodies. Because the force of gravity is so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weak between these particles, this can only happen \nif they’re moving very slowly relative to each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287720,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other. Larger relative speeds they’ll just wizz \npast each other before gravity can do its thing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294640,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that a system of particles with large \nand random relative speeds is dynamically hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300280,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "otherwise it’s dynamically cold. But \neven if a ring system starts out hot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305120,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collisions between particles can slow \nrelative speeds to the point where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they do start to coalesce gravitationally. \nAt that point they can build moons. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315120,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collisions can’t reduce particles speeds then \nthe system remains hot and also remains a ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322040,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if a ring system cools,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s still a way for it to stay a ring. \nFor example, Saturn’s rings are dynamically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cold in fact, they may have the minimum possible \ninternal energy that they could possibly have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other thing that can keep a ring a \nring is the gravitational field of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet itself. Let’s Imagine some particles just \nstarting to clump together into a ring system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the grand ambition of forming a new moon. \nAs the clump grows, the mutual gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353680,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field holding it together gets stronger. But \nthe planet also has a gravitational field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that field is stronger the closer you get to \nthe planet. That means particles in our growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365720,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clump on the side nearer to the planet feel a \nstronger inward pull than those on the far side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372000,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As long as we place this body far from the planet, \nthat gravity differential is be weaker than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity force holding the clump together. But the \ncloser to the planet we place it, the stronger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this differential becomes. Within a certain \nrange, the difference in the force of gravity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the body’s width is stronger than the force \nof gravity holding the body together. That causes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394920,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the growing clump to breaks apart—or to not grow \nin the first place. This differential force is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401880,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called a tidal force and our Moon’s differential \npull on Earth’s oceans is the cause of tides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409800,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can have rings if the material is \ndynamically hot OR if the material is inside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416320,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this critical distance for tidal disruption. That \ndistance is called the Roche limit, and it depends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423960,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a few factors, like the density of material \nforming the moon and whether the material is held  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429120,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together by anything other than gravity. Within \nthe Roche limit for a given planet and a given  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435080,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material, Moons aren’t possible and so orbiting \nparticles will persist as rings. Beyond the Roche  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limits, these particles will eventually form moons \nas long as they can also become dynamically cool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448240,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For Saturn, the Roche limit for ice is around \n140 thousand kilometres from the planet’s centre.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are no moons inside this boundary, and there \nis only very thin ring material outside of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461560,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two main ways for a planet to gain \nrings. One is to capture small particles that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468800,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrive elsewhere in the solar system, \nwhich then fail to coalesce into moons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example due to tidal forces. Or a larger \nobject could be shattered into smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480120,
    "dDurationMs": 9240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles while orbiting or on a near fly-by—again \nperhaps by tidal forces or through a collision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489360,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other possible scenarios, like a \ngiant impact with the planet itself, spraying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles from the planet into a ring system. \nSupposedly that’s how Earth’s moon was formed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although in this case the “ring system” didn’t \nlast long because it was outside Earth’s Roche  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limit. But in principle, Earth could acquire \na persistent ring system as long as it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512200,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within its Roche limit. So let’s look at the new \nevidence that this was, in fact, once the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518760,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve known for some time that around 4 and half \nMillion years ago a bunch of stuff fell to Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525920,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space rocks are peppering our planet constantly, \nand we see their remains—both in impact craters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also in more broadly dispersed \nmeteorite material in sedimentary rock,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such as limestone. In fact, from these rocks \nwe can trace the flux of meteorites—the rate of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544360,
    "dDurationMs": 9000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infall. And for a period of around 40 million \nyears starting around 466 million years ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553360,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sediment became enriched with a type of space \nrock called L-chondrite. Enriched by a factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560320,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 100-1000. So, for this stretch of some tens of \nmillions of years during the Ordovician period,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568600,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sky blazed with shooting stars … for some \nreason. This is the Ordovician impact spike"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576600,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most obvious explanation is that \nthere was some massive influx of material into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inner solar system from the asteroid belt. You \nexpect that sort of thing when two large asteroids  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588600,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collide. In fact, that was, and perhaps, for the \ntime being, still is the leading hypothesis for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594880,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the excess L-chondrite deposits. Except there’s \nsomething that doesn’t quite hang together. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601800,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this event really did occur in the asteroid belt, \nwe’d expect all inner solar system bodies to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608640,
    "dDurationMs": 9280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "affected. But there’s no evidence of an increased \nimpact rate on Mars or the Moon from this period."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This incongruity is part of what led the \nresearchers from Monash university to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look into this idea that the many, many smallish \nmeteorites that mark the Ordovician impact spike  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629680,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were produced close to the Earth \ninstead of in the asteroid belt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So here’s their story: about 466 million years \nago, a large asteroid – about 10 kilometres  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across – had a near miss with the Earth. It \npassed just thousands of kilometers above the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647960,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet’s surface, below the Roche limit, \nwhere extreme tidal forces disrupted the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "body. Some of the resulting cloud of debris \nwas then captured by the Earth’s gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first, these particles would have slung around \nthe Earth on very elliptical orbits and with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666160,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range of inclinations. At first, the scattered \norbital orientations led to frequent collisions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but those collisions circularized the \norbits and helped flattened them into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677920,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a disk. Earth’s slight equatorial bulge \nalso creates a preferential orbital plane  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the equator, further stabilizing \norbits into a very flat ring system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now some particles ended up outside \nthe Roche limit may have temporarily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694440,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed small moons out there. But tidal \nforces would have caused them to migrate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inwards until they were once again tidally \ndisrupted and rejoined the inner ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706320,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that ring could not last forever. Earth’s \nRoche limit is a few thousand kilometers for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712840,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid rock asteroid and up to 15 or so thousand \nclicks for a fragmented asteroid—a “rubble pile”.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720440,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either way, an object that breaks up with this \nrange is also largely inside Earth’s exosphere.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728320,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the extremely diffuse region where the \natmosphere transitions into interplanetary space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734280,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The tiny drag caused by interactions with the \nexosphere would have caused these ring orbits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to slowly decay. One by one, ring particles \nwould find themselves in an accelerating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spiral into the thickening atmosphere, \nending in a fiery plummet to the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if this story is right, then \nfor about 40 million years the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757840,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth had a spectacular ring stretching \nthousands of kilometers above the equator,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also a constant rain of shooting stars \nfrom the base of that slowly draining ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well cool story. But how do we test something \nlike this? How do we tell that this stuff that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fell from space came from a ring rather than from \na collision in the asteroid belt, given that it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782440,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would all be the same type of material? Well the \ntrick is to see where the stuff landed. Material  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming direction from the asteroid belt should \nland relatively randomly over the entire surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the globe. On the other hand, material falling \nfrom a ring should land close to the equator."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not exactly easy to say where space rocks \nlanded half a billion years ago. The tiny bits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of L-chondrite found in sedimentary layers have \ndispersed widely from their atmospheric entry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813840,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points. But there are several actual craters \nthought to be caused by larger impacts from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820120,
    "dDurationMs": 8200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this event. And these are scattered across the \nglobe, from Europe to North America to Australia,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828320,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so definitely not equatorial. But remember that \nthese craters formed a half billion years ago,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836200,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the continents were nowhere \nnear their current locations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the researchers traced the tectonic motion of \nthese impact craters back in time to see where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846360,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were located when they were formed. And \nyes, it’s crazy that we can do that. Back then,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852800,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most of Earth’s solid surface was clustered in \nthe southern hemisphere supercontinent Gondwana."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858920,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the impact locations? All of them within \nroughly 30 degrees latitude of the equator.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just what you’d expect from a collapsed planetary \nring. But let’s not get ahead of ourselves. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only 21 craters, and the range is around 60 \ndegrees latitude. Maybe there really was an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880600,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influx of rocks from the asteroid belt, and all \nthe bigger ones just got lucky and landed in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tropics by accident. What are the chances? Well \nthe team calculate the chances. The likelihood  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of getting all of these impacts within this \nband around the equator is reported as one in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899200,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "25 million. If they really were from a global \nrandom distribution that is. Therefore,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906040,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these researchers argue that it’s much \nmore likely that these impacts came from a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909680,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reservoir of space rocks localised above the \nequator of the Earth. AKA a planetary ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems … plausible. Not super easy \nto test,  unfortunately and I'll come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920920,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to how we might test this hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923680,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it does feel reasonable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926480,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the idea of a planetary ring does at \nleast seem consistent with the general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931800,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state of affairs 2.6 hundred million years \nago. So then animal life was all marine at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939560,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that time during the Ordovician period, \nwas busily complexifying in preparation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946000,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for its foray onto land. And that land was \nclustered in the Gondwana supercontinent,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which spent most of this era covered in \nprimitive plantlife. But towards the end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958480,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "became a colossal ice-cap as Earth plunged \ninto the Hirnantian glaciation. This ice  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "age was so bad that it led to the second \nlargest mass extinction in Earth’s history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Ordovician impact spike came just before \nthe Hirnantian glaciation, and others have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposed that the asteroid influx precipitated \nthe cooling by depositing a lot of reflective  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dust in the atmosphere. But the planetary ring \nhypothesis may provide an even more compelling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanation for the cooling. That’s because \nan equatorial ring would provide a sunshade  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for whichever hemisphere happened to be in \nwinter. That would result in harsher winters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001880,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and potentially initiate the runaway growth of \nGondwana glaciers that precipitated the ice age ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by the way, if you want a deeper look \ninto what would happen to the modern Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it had rings, check out Joe Scott’s episode \nwhere he explores exactly that. And in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "process produced amazing visualizations \nof what a ringed Earth would look like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026200,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of which we borrowed for this episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So did the ancient Earth have a ring system? \nWe can’t say one way or the other just yet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034560,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors suggest various ways \nto strengthen the hypothesis or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refute it. The most obvious thing is to \nfind more impact craters from this era,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and refine our dating of the meteorite material \nin those craters, and of the mineral similarity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this material. But if we found craters of the \nsame type of meteorite and time very far from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056200,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equator it would put this hypothesis \nin doubt, but if we keep finding sibling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "craters that were close to the Ordovician \nequator then things look more promising."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if it does turn out that Earth had a ring \nsystem a half billion years ago, then there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074120,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no reason we can’t have one again. All we need \nis a horrifyingly near-miss with an asteroid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "followed by millions of years of intense meteor \nactivity and a cataclysmic ice age. But it's a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small price to pay to firmly establish Earth as \nthe most spectacular world in local space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095240,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we go, I wanted to let \nyou know that today’s episode was part of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth Month on PBS. Every April, PBS releases \na lot of new episodes celebrating our amazing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104960,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet. There’s going to be lots of great \nscience content and links to their latest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109000,
    "dDurationMs": 29720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode and the full Earth Month \nplaylist - are in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
